  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through next Friday)\nIssued at 1150 PM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nAn upper level ridge is expected to stay over the \ncombined Panhandles into Monday. However, this time falling under \nsouthwest flow which is expected to lead to increase Pacific \nmoisture transporting up through El Paso and into the FA. Another \nafternoon with highs expected in the mid to upper 80s is progged for \nMonday. This will all change with an approaching upper level trough \nfrom the W to NW. At the same time the remnants of Hurricane Polo \nis expected to be a depression centered near El Paso by Wed morning. \nThis will add to the available moisture being pumped into Parts of \nNM and West Texas. Though PoPs begin to return Mon afternoon/night, \nPoPs are expected increase going into Tue night into Wed. Currently \nthe NBM is giving PoPs in the 70-80 range starting Tue afternoon and \nlasting into Wed morning. LREF probs for one inch of rain across the \nFA are near 30% while the NBM probs for at least one inch of rain in \n24 hours is as high as 60%. Shifts in the projected track of the \nupper trough coming from the west and Polo may shift where the best \nrain falls, but current signals giving some hope to further decrease \nthe rain deficits seen across the combined Panhandles this year. \n\nGoing into Wed afternoon as this aforementioned systems move off, \nPoPs begin to drop to 30 late Wed then near 20 for Thu. Thanks to \nthe increase in cloud cover and rain, Tue into Fri afternoon highs \nare expected to be in the 70s to lower 80s.",
